A Global Innovation Trajectory and Forecasting Engine (GIFT)

Theoretical Foundations for Identifiable Latent State Estimation and Probabilistic Forecasting of Technology Emergence

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek 

Abstract

This dissertation establishes the theoretical and methodological foundations for a Global Innovation Trajectory and Forecasting Engine (GIFT), a formal probabilistic framework for detecting, modeling, and forecasting technological innovation through the integration of heterogeneous evidence streams. The central contribution is a rigorous mathematical treatment of technology emergence as a latent dynamic process, inferable from noisy, multi-source observations under explicitly stated identifiability conditions. We prove that the latent capability state of a technology is identifiable up to component-wise invertible transformations when conditioned on sufficiently variable auxiliary covariates, extending the theory of identifiable variational autoencoders to the domain of technology dynamics. We further establish a principled separation between capability trajectories and attention trajectories, providing a theoretically grounded mechanism for de-biasing hype-cycle dynamics. The framework integrates this latent state estimation with a temporally evolving technology graph and a hazard-based forecasting engine, formalized through complete algorithmic specifications. A pre-registered experimental protocol is defined for the battery technology domain, incorporating rigorous baselines including human forecasters and large language models, with explicit provisions for preventing data leakage and testing core theoretical conjectures. This work provides the theoretical groundwork for subsequent empirical implementation, establishing conditions under which learned representations of technology state can be considered meaningful and forecasts can be considered calibrated.

---

1. Introduction

Human technological evolution constitutes a high-dimensional, networked, partially observable dynamical system. The capacity to anticipate technological emergence, acceleration, and convergence carries profound implications for research policy, investment strategy, and strategic planning. Yet the operationalization of technology forecasting has historically relied upon heuristic scoring methods and ad-hoc indicator combinations, yielding results of variable reliability and limited theoretical justification. The IARPA FUSE program, despite substantial investment and methodological sophistication, demonstrated the profound difficulty of establishing ground truth for emergence and the variability of performance across forecasting approaches. This dissertation addresses these limitations through the construction of a theoretically rigorous, identifiable, probabilistic framework.

The central hypothesis of this work is that technological innovation can be modeled as a latent dynamic system, where the true state of a technology—its underlying capability, maturity, and potential—is not directly observable but can be recovered from heterogeneous evidence streams under explicitly stated identifiability conditions. By fusing diverse signals into a theoretically identifiable model of latent state and its interactions, the framework aims to produce calibrated, actionable probabilistic forecasts of technology emergence, acceleration, and convergence.

The primary theoretical contributions are fourfold. First, we establish formal conditions under which the latent capability state of a technology is identifiable from observational data up to component-wise invertible transformations. This result, extending the theory of identifiable variational autoencoders to the domain of technology dynamics, provides the foundational guarantee that the learned representations are meaningful features rather than arbitrary functions of the data. Second, we formalize the separation between a technology's fundamental capability trajectory and its societal attention trajectory, providing a principled mechanism for de-biasing hype-cycle dynamics that confound naive forecasting approaches. Third, we specify a complete algorithmic architecture integrating latent state estimation, dynamic graph construction, and survival analysis-based forecasting, formalized in reproducible pseudocode suitable for empirical implementation. Fourth, we define a pre-registered experimental protocol designed to test the framework's core conjectures under conditions that prevent common pitfalls such as data leakage and overfitting.

The remainder of this dissertation is organized as follows. Section 2 establishes mathematical notation and core definitions. Section 3 reviews the relevant literature across technology forecasting, identifiable latent variable models, and survival analysis. Section 4 presents the foundational identifiability theorem, including the complete formal statement and proof. Section 5 formalizes the dynamic state-space model and the variational inference objective. Section 6 specifies the algorithmic blueprints for the system. Section 7 details the pre-registered experimental protocol. Section 8 discusses theoretical limitations and open problems. Section 9 concludes.

