2. Mathematical Foundations and Core Definitions

Let time be discrete, indexed by $t \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$. We consider a finite, potentially growing set of technology entities $\mathcal{T} = \{\tau_1, \tau_2, \ldots, \tau_N\}$, where the ontological status of these entities is discussed in Section 8. All random variables are defined on a probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P})$.

Definition 2.1 (Observable Signal Vector). For each technology $\tau_i$ at time $t$, we observe a vector of $m$ signals:

Y_i(t) = (y_{i1}(t), y_{i2}(t), \ldots, y_{im}(t)) \in \mathbb{R}^m

where each component represents a distinct data source (publication counts, patent filings, funding events, performance metrics). We formally partition these signals into two disjoint sets:

Y_i(t) = (Y_i^{(cap)}(t), Y_i^{(att)}(t))

where $Y_i^{(cap)}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{m_c}$ are capability signals—proxies for performance, cost, or efficiency—and $Y_i^{(att)}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{m_a}$ are attention signals—proxies for societal interest, funding announcements, news volume—with $m_c + m_a = m$. This separation constitutes a core theoretical construct of the framework.

Definition 2.2 (Latent State Vector). For each technology $\tau_i$ at time $t$, there exists an unobserved state vector $Z_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^d$ representing the true multi-dimensional maturity of the technology. This state governs the generation of capability signals. The components of $Z_i(t)$ are not assumed to have fixed semantic interpretation; their structure is determined by the identifiability constraints introduced in Section 4.

Definition 2.3 (Temporal Technology Graph). At each time $t$, the relationships between technologies are represented by a directed, weighted graph $G_t = (V_t, E_t, W_t)$ where $V_t \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ is the set of active technologies, $E_t \subseteq V_t \times V_t$ is the set of edges representing dependencies, and $W_t: E_t \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ assigns non-negative weights to edges. The formal construction of this graph is specified in Section 6.

Definition 2.4 (Auxiliary Covariates). Let $U_i(t) \in \mathbb{R}^p$ be a vector of technology-specific or global exogenous variables that may influence the latent state but are not generated by it, including macroeconomic indices, raw material prices, and regulatory stringency scores. These variables play a crucial role in the identifiability theorem by anchoring the latent space.

Definition 2.5 (Information History). The history of all observations up to time $t$ is denoted by:

\mathcal{D}_t = \{Y_i(s), G_s, U_i(s) : 1 \le i \le N, 0 \le s \le t\}

Definition 2.6 (Graph Neighborhood). For technology $i$ at time $t$, the set of graph-based neighbors is defined as:

\mathcal{N}_i(t) = \{j \in V_t : (j, i) \in E_t\}

representing technologies that directly influence technology $i$ according to the graph structure.

