3. Related Work

This dissertation engages three distinct bodies of literature: technology forecasting and emergence detection, identifiable latent variable models, and survival analysis for event prediction.

3.1 Technology Forecasting and Emergence Detection

The systematic study of technology emergence has produced diverse methodological approaches. Porter et al. (2019) documented the IARPA FUSE program's efforts to operationalize emergence detection, revealing the profound difficulty of establishing ground truth and the variability of performance across forecasting approaches. Their findings underscore the necessity of rigorous, theoretically grounded alternatives to heuristic scoring methods.

Quantitative approaches to technology forecasting have included bibliometric indicators, patent analysis, and trend extrapolation. Magee, Basnet, Funk, and Benson (2016) examined quantitative empirical trends in technical performance, establishing empirical regularities in technology improvement rates. Dedehayir and Steinert (2016) analyzed hype cycles in emerging technologies, demonstrating the confounding effects of attention dynamics on perceived technology maturity—a phenomenon our framework explicitly addresses through capability-attention separation.

Graph-based approaches have gained prominence for modeling technology relationships. Zhu and Motohashi (2022) employed graph convolutional networks for identifying technology convergence using patent text information, demonstrating the utility of graph-structured representations for technology dynamics. The GIFT framework extends these approaches by integrating graph structure with theoretically identifiable latent variable models.

3.2 Identifiable Latent Variable Models

The problem of latent variable identifiability has a substantial history in statistics and machine learning. Classical results established that linear independent component analysis is identifiable under non-Gaussianity assumptions, but the general nonlinear case remained unresolved until recent advances.

Hyvärinen and Morioka (2017) demonstrated that nonlinear ICA could be made identifiable by exploiting temporal structure through contrastive learning. This temporal structure approach has been extended to various settings, including nonstationary time series and hidden Markov models. However, these approaches typically require access to the true temporal generative process, which may not be available for technology data.

A more relevant line of work concerns identifiable variational autoencoders. Khemakhem et al. (2020) proved that VAEs with conditionally factorial exponential family priors achieve identifiability up to component-wise transformations when conditioned on sufficiently variable auxiliary variables. This result is the direct theoretical foundation for our Theorem 4.1. Hyvärinen, Khemakhem, and Morioka (2023) provide a comprehensive review of recent advances in nonlinear ICA for principled disentanglement in unsupervised deep learning, situating the identifiability theory within broader deep learning practice.

The application of identifiable latent variable models to technology forecasting represents a novel contribution of this dissertation. While prior work has employed VAEs for technology representation, the theoretical guarantees established here are—to our knowledge—unprecedented in this domain.

3.3 Survival Analysis for Event Prediction

Survival analysis provides the statistical framework for modeling time-to-event data with censoring. The Cox proportional hazards model (Cox, 1972) remains the foundational approach, offering semi-parametric estimation under proportional hazards assumptions. Grambsch and Therneau (1994) developed diagnostic tests for the proportional hazards assumption based on Schoenfeld residuals, which we incorporate into our forecasting algorithm.

Machine learning extensions of survival analysis have addressed limitations of the proportional hazards framework. DeepSurv (Katzman et al., 2018) extends the Cox model with neural network architectures while maintaining the proportional hazards structure. Random survival forests (Ishwaran et al., 2008) provide a nonparametric alternative that does not impose proportional hazards. The GIFT framework integrates these methods with latent state estimation, employing diagnostic-driven model selection.

The application of survival analysis to technology forecasting has precedent in the literature, though typically with simpler feature representations. Our contribution lies in the integration with identifiable latent states and graph-based features, providing theoretically grounded inputs to the survival model.

