5. Dynamic State-Space Model and Inference

This section formalizes the dynamic components of the GIFT framework, including the explicit state-space equations, the variational inference objective, and the connection between the latent state and the time-to-event forecasting model.

5.1 Dynamic Latent State and Observation Model

Assumption 5.1 (Conditional Markovian Transition). The latent state of technology $i$ evolves according to a Markov process influenced by the latent states of its graph-based neighbors $\mathcal{N}_i(t)$ and auxiliary covariates $U_i(t)$:

Z_i(t+1) = f_i\left(Z_i(t), Z_{\mathcal{N}_i(t)}(t), U_i(t)\right) + \eta_i(t)

where $f_i: \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^{d|\mathcal{N}_i(t)|} \times \mathbb{R}^p \to \mathbb{R}^d$ is a transition function and $\eta_i(t)$ is zero-mean process noise. For the identifiability result to apply at each time step, we require that the conditional prior $p(Z_i(t+1) \mid Z_i(t), Z_{\mathcal{N}_i(t)}(t), U_i(t))$ belongs to a conditionally factorial exponential family, extending Assumption 4.1 to the dynamic setting.

Assumption 5.2 (Conditional Observation Model). The observable capability and attention signals are generated via separate functions from the latent state and auxiliary variables:

Y_i^{(cap)}(t) = h_i^{(cap)}(Z_i(t), U_i(t)) + \epsilon_i^{(cap)}(t)

Y_i^{(att)}(t) = h_i^{(att)}(U_i(t), \mathcal{D}_{t-1}) + \epsilon_i^{(att)}(t)

where $h_i^{(cap)}$ and $h_i^{(att)}$ are measurement functions and $\epsilon_i^{(\cdot)}$ are zero-mean observation noises. The attention model is not a function of the current latent state $Z_i(t)$, reflecting that attention responds to past observed capability and external factors with a delay. This asymmetry captures the temporal structure of hype cycles: capability advances precede attention surges, not vice versa.

5.2 Amortized Variational Inference Objective

To estimate the posterior distribution over the latent states $Z_i(t)$ given observations $\mathcal{D}_t$, we employ amortized variational inference. We parameterize an approximate posterior $q_\phi(Z_i(t) \mid \mathcal{D}_t)$ using a neural network encoder and a generative model $p_\theta(Y_i(t) \mid Z_i(t), U_i(t))$ using a neural network decoder. The transition function $f_i$ is parameterized by a prior network $p_\psi(Z_i(t+1) \mid Z_i(t), Z_{\mathcal{N}_i(t)}(t), U_i(t))$.

The objective is to maximize the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO) over the full dataset of sequences. For a single technology sequence of length $T$:

\mathcal{L}_{\text{ELBO}}(\theta, \phi, \psi; \mathcal{D}_T) = \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi(Z_t \mid \mathcal{D}_t)}\left[\log p_\theta(Y_t \mid Z_t, U_t)\right] - \sum_{t=1}^T D_{\text{KL}}\left(q_\phi(Z_t \mid \mathcal{D}_t) \| p_\psi(Z_t \mid Z_{t-1}, Z_{\mathcal{N}_i(t-1)}(t-1), U_t, G_{t-1})\right)

This result follows from Jensen's inequality applied to the log-marginal likelihood $\log p(Y_{1:T})$:

\log p(Y_{1:T}) = \log \int \prod_{t=1}^T p_\theta(Y_t \mid Z_t, U_t) p_\psi(Z_t \mid Z_{t-1}, \mathcal{D}_{t-1}) \, dZ_{1:T}

= \log \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi(Z_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{D}_T)} \left[\frac{\prod_{t=1}^T p_\theta(Y_t \mid Z_t, U_t) p_\psi(Z_t \mid Z_{t-1}, \mathcal{D}_{t-1})}{q_\phi(Z_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{D}_T)}\right]

\ge \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi(Z_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{D}_T)} \left[\log \prod_{t=1}^T p_\theta(Y_t \mid Z_t, U_t) + \log \prod_{t=1}^T p_\psi(Z_t \mid Z_{t-1}, \mathcal{D}_{t-1}) - \log q_\phi(Z_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{D}_T)\right]

By factorizing the approximate posterior $q_\phi(Z_{1:T} \mid \mathcal{D}_T) = \prod_{t=1}^T q_\phi(Z_t \mid \mathcal{D}_t)$, we obtain the stated ELBO. The KL divergence term regularizes the approximate posterior to be close to the transition prior, which encodes the graph-based dynamics.

5.3 From Latent State to Forecast Events via Hazard Models

The link between the continuous latent state and the discrete event of technology emergence is formalized through survival analysis.

Definition 5.1 (Forecast Event). For a technology $i$ and a pre-specified scalar metric $Q_i(t)$ derived from the capability signals $Y_i^{(cap)}(t)$, we define the event $E_i$ as the first time $T_i$ when $Q_i(t)$ crosses a predefined threshold $Q^*$:

T_i = \inf\{t \ge 0 : Q_i(t) \ge Q^*\}

The choice of $Q_i(t)$ and $Q^*$ is specified in the experimental protocol (Section 7).

Definition 5.2 (Hazard Function). The hazard function $\lambda_i(t)$ represents the instantaneous rate of event occurrence at time $t$, conditional on no prior occurrence. We model the hazard using a Cox proportional hazards model as a rigorous, interpretable baseline:

\lambda_i(t) = \lambda_0(t) \exp\left(\beta^\top X_i(t) + \gamma^\top G_i(t) + \delta^\top U_i(t)\right)

where $\lambda_0(t)$ is a non-parametric baseline hazard, $X_i(t)$ is a vector of features derived from the estimated latent state $\mathbb{E}[Z_i(t) \mid \mathcal{D}_t]$ and its dynamics (including estimated growth rates), $G_i(t)$ is a vector of graph-based features (including network centrality and convergence indicators), and $U_i(t)$ are external covariates.

The survival probability and the probability of an event before a horizon $H$ are:

S_i(t) = \exp\left(-\int_0^t \lambda_i(\tau) \, d\tau\right)

P(T_i \le H \mid \mathcal{D}_t) = 1 - \exp\left(-\int_t^H \lambda_i(\tau) \, d\tau\right)

We acknowledge that the proportional hazards assumption may be violated. The framework includes a diagnostic step (Section 6, Algorithm 6.3) and a fallback to more flexible models should the assumption be rejected.

