6. Algorithmic Blueprints

This section provides formal, reproducible algorithmic specifications for the core modules of the GIFT framework. Each algorithm is presented in pseudocode format suitable for implementation.

Algorithm 6.1: Technology Entity Resolution

Purpose: Construct the initial set of technology entities $\mathcal{T}$ from a corpus of documents.

Input: Document collection $\mathcal{D} = \{d_1, \dots, d_M\}$ with text $T_d$ and metadata $M_d$; scientific embedding model $\mathcal{M}_{\text{embed}}$ (SciBERT/SPECTER); HDBSCAN parameters; Jaccard term overlap threshold $\theta_{\text{Jaccard}}$; graph connectivity threshold $\theta_{\text{graph}}$; temporal divergence threshold $\delta_{\text{temp}}$; expert validation sample size $S$.

Output: Set of canonical technology entities $\mathcal{T} = \{\tau_1, \dots, \tau_N\}$ and document mapping $\mu: \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{T}$.

Procedure:

1. Step 1: Dense Text Embedding & Initial Spatial Clustering
   · For each document $d \in \mathcal{D}$: compute embedding $\mathbf{e}_d \leftarrow \mathcal{M}_{\text{embed}}(T_d) \in \mathbb{R}^k$.
   · Reduce dimensionality: $\mathbf{U} \leftarrow \text{UMAP}(\{\mathbf{e}_d\}_{d=1}^M)$.
   · Cluster: $\mathcal{C}_{\text{init}} \leftarrow \text{HDBSCAN}(\mathbf{U}, \text{min\_cluster\_size})$.
2. Step 2: Term Extraction & Graph-Based Cluster Disambiguation
   · For each cluster $C_k \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{init}}$: extract top TF-IDF terms $K(C_k)$.
   · Build inter-cluster citation/co-occurrence graph $G_{\mathcal{C}}$.
   · Initialize $\mathcal{C}_{\text{merged}} \leftarrow \mathcal{C}_{\text{init}}$.
   · For each pair of clusters $(C_a, C_b)$ with $a \neq b$:
     · Compute Jaccard similarity $J(C_a, C_b)$ and graph weight $W(C_a, C_b)$.
     · If $J \ge \theta_{\text{Jaccard}}$ and $W \ge \theta_{\text{graph}}$, merge $C_a$ and $C_b$.
3. Step 3: Temporal Divergence Splitting
   · For each cluster $C \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{merged}}$:
     · Partition $C$ into time-windowed sub-collections.
     · Compute embedding centroid trajectory and its variance $\sigma_{\text{traj}}^2$.
     · If $\sigma_{\text{traj}}^2 > \delta_{\text{temp}}$, split $C$ into two sub-clusters via bisection K-means.
4. Step 4: Manual Expert Validation & Mapping
   · Sample $S$ clusters for expert review; compute Cohen's $\kappa$.
   · If $\kappa < 0.75$, adjust thresholds and repeat.
   · Assign entity IDs and define mapping $\mu$.

Algorithm 6.2: Amortized Variational Inference for Dynamic Latent State Estimation

Purpose: Train the CVAE model to estimate latent technology states.

Input: Dataset of multi-source signal sequences $\{Y_i(1:T)\}_{i=1}^N$, exogenous covariates $\{U_i(1:T)\}_{i=1}^N$, dynamic technology graphs $\{G_t\}_{t=1}^T$; learning rate $\eta$; batch size $B$; latent dimension $d$.

Output: Optimized variational inference encoder $q_\phi$, observation decoder $p_\theta$, prior transition network $p_\psi$, and latent state posterior estimates $\hat{Z}_i(t)$.

Procedure:

1. Initialize parameters $\phi$, $\theta$, $\psi$.
2. While not converged:
   · Sample mini-batch $\mathcal{B} \subset \{1, \dots, N\}$ with $|\mathcal{B}| = B$.
   · Initialize $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ELBO}} = 0$.
   · For each technology $i \in \mathcal{B}$:
     · For each time step $t = 1 \dots T$:
       · Extract in-neighbors $\mathcal{N}_i(t-1)$ from $G_{t-1}$.
       · Encoder: $[\boldsymbol{\mu}_\phi(t), \boldsymbol{\sigma}_\phi^2(t)] \leftarrow q_\phi(Y_i(1:t), U_i(1:t), G_{1:t-1})$.
       · Reparameterization: Sample $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I_d)$; set $Z_i(t) = \boldsymbol{\mu}_\phi(t) + \boldsymbol{\sigma}_\phi(t) \odot \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_t$.
       · Prior transition: If $t=1$, set prior to $\mathcal{N}(0, I_d)$; else $[\boldsymbol{\mu}_\psi(t), \boldsymbol{\sigma}_\psi^2(t)] \leftarrow p_\psi(Z_i(t-1), Z_{\mathcal{N}_i(t-1)}(t-1), U_i(t))$.
       · Reconstruction loss: $\log p_\theta(Y_i(t) \mid Z_i(t), U_i(t))$.
       · KL divergence: $D_{\text{KL}}(q_\phi \| p_\psi) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{j=1}^d \left[ \log \frac{\sigma_{\psi,j}^2}{\sigma_{\phi,j}^2} + \frac{\sigma_{\phi,j}^2 + (\mu_{\phi,j} - \mu_{\psi,j})^2}{\sigma_{\psi,j}^2} - 1 \right]$.
       · Accumulate: $\mathcal{L}_{\text{ELBO}} \leftarrow \mathcal{L}_{\text{ELBO}} + \log p_\theta - D_{\text{KL}}$.
   · Compute gradient of $-\frac{1}{B}\mathcal{L}_{\text{ELBO}}$ w.r.t. $(\phi, \theta, \psi)$; update with Adam.
3. After training, extract posterior means $\hat{Z}_i(t) = \boldsymbol{\mu}_\phi(t)$.

Algorithm 6.3: Diagnostic-Driven Survival Forecast

Purpose: Perform forecasting with explicit model selection based on assumption diagnostics.

Input: Inferred latent state means $\{\hat{Z}_i(1:t)\}$, signal histories $\mathcal{D}_t$, target metric threshold $Q^*$, forecast horizon $H$, significance level $\alpha = 0.05$, bootstrap iterations $B_s$.

Output: Model choice $\mathcal{M}^*$, event probability point estimate $\hat{P}(T_i \le H \mid \mathcal{D}_t)$, 90% credible intervals $[P_{\text{low}}, P_{\text{high}}]$.

Procedure:

1. Feature Vector & Event Target Construction
   · For each technology $i$:
     · Compute growth rate $g_i(t)$ and acceleration $a_i(t)$.
     · Extract graph centrality $C_{\text{graph},i}(t)$, bottleneck vector $B_i(t)$, attention vector $x_i^{(\text{att})}(t)$.
     · Assemble $X_i(t) = [\hat{Z}_i(t), g_i(t), a_i(t), C_{\text{graph},i}(t), B_i(t), x_i^{(\text{att})}(t)]$.
     · Compute event time $T_i$ and censoring flag $\delta_i$.
   · Form training set $\mathcal{D}_{\text{surv}} = \{(X_i(t), T_i, \delta_i)\}$.
2. Baseline Model Fitting & Proportional Hazards Diagnostics
   · Fit Cox PH model $\mathcal{M}_{\text{Cox}}$.
   · Compute Schoenfeld residuals and Grambsch-Therneau test; let $p_{\min}$ be the minimum p-value.
   · If $p_{\min} \ge \alpha$: select $\mathcal{M}^* = \mathcal{M}_{\text{Cox}}$.
   · Else:
     · Train DeepSurv and Random Survival Forest.
     · Evaluate Brier scores; select model with lowest Brier score as $\mathcal{M}^*$.
3. Probabilistic Point Forecast
   · Estimate survival curve $\hat{S}_{\mathcal{M}^*}(s \mid X_i(t))$ for $s \in [t, t+H]$.
   · Compute $\hat{P}(T_i \le H \mid \mathcal{D}_t) = 1 - \hat{S}_{\mathcal{M}^*}(t+H)$.
4. Bootstrap Uncertainty Quantification
   · For $b = 1 \dots B_s$:
     · Draw bootstrap sample, refit $\mathcal{M}^*$, compute $\hat{P}^{(b)}$.
   · Report 5th and 95th percentiles.

