Appendix C: Algorithmic Blueprints (Full Detail)

We present the complete algorithmic specifications in a format suitable for implementation. These algorithms correspond to those in Section 6 but include additional technical details.

Algorithm C.1: Technology Entity Resolution

Input: Document collection $\mathcal{D} = \{d_1, \dots, d_M\}$; embedding model $\mathcal{M}_{\text{embed}}$; UMAP parameters; HDBSCAN parameters; thresholds $\theta_{\text{Jaccard}}$, $\theta_{\text{graph}}$, $\delta_{\text{temp}}$; sample size $S$.

Output: Technology entities $\mathcal{T}$ and mapping $\mu$.

1. Embedding: For each $d \in \mathcal{D}$, compute $\mathbf{e}_d \leftarrow \mathcal{M}_{\text{embed}}(T_d)$.
2. Dimensionality reduction: $\mathbf{U} \leftarrow \text{UMAP}(\{\mathbf{e}_d\})$.
3. Initial clustering: $\mathcal{C}_{\text{init}} \leftarrow \text{HDBSCAN}(\mathbf{U}, \text{min\_cluster\_size})$.
4. Term extraction: For each cluster $C \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{init}}$, extract top 20 TF-IDF terms $K(C)$.
5. Graph construction: Build co-occurrence/citation graph among clusters: nodes are clusters, edge weight $W(C_a, C_b)$ = number of documents citing between clusters.
6. Merging: Set $\mathcal{C}_{\text{merged}} \leftarrow \mathcal{C}_{\text{init}}$. Repeatedly scan all pairs; if $J(C_a,C_b) \ge \theta_{\text{Jaccard}}$ and $W(C_a,C_b) \ge \theta_{\text{graph}}$, merge them. Repeat until no merges.
7. Temporal splitting: For each cluster $C \in \mathcal{C}_{\text{merged}}$:
   · Partition $C$ into yearly sub-collections $C^{(t)}$ for $t = t_{\min}, \ldots, t_{\max}$.
   · Compute centroid $\bar{\mathbf{e}}^{(t)} = \frac{1}{|C^{(t)}|} \sum_{d \in C^{(t)}} \mathbf{e}_d$.
   · Compute trajectory variance $\sigma_{\text{traj}}^2 = \text{Var}(\{\bar{\mathbf{e}}^{(t)}\})$.
   · If $\sigma_{\text{traj}}^2 > \delta_{\text{temp}}$, split $C$ into two sub-clusters by K-means ($k=2$) on all documents in $C$.
8. Expert validation: Randomly sample $S$ clusters. Two experts label each cluster as coherent or not. Compute Cohen's $\kappa$. If $\kappa < 0.75$, adjust thresholds and repeat steps 6–7.
9. Entity assignment: Assign unique IDs to final clusters; define $\mu(d) = \tau$ if $d \in C_\tau$.

Algorithm C.2: Amortized Variational Inference for Dynamic Latent State Estimation

Input: Signal sequences $\{Y_i(1:T)\}$, covariates $\{U_i(1:T)\}$, graphs $\{G_t\}$; hyperparameters $\eta$, $B$, $d$, $k$.

Output: Trained encoder $q_\phi$, decoder $p_\theta$, prior $p_\psi$, and posterior means $\hat{Z}_i(t)$.

1. Initialize $\phi, \theta, \psi$.
2. Training loop: While not converged:
   · Sample mini-batch $\mathcal{B}$ of size $B$.
   · $\mathcal{L} \leftarrow 0$.
   · For each $i \in \mathcal{B}$:
     · For $t = 1$ to $T$:
       · Get neighbors $\mathcal{N}_i(t-1)$ from $G_{t-1}$.
       · Posterior inference: $[\mu_\phi(t), \sigma_\phi^2(t)] \leftarrow q_\phi(Y_i(1:t), U_i(1:t), G_{1:t-1})$.
       · Sample $\epsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0,I_d)$; $Z_i(t) = \mu_\phi(t) + \sigma_\phi(t) \odot \epsilon$.
       · Prior: If $t=1$: $[\mu_\psi, \sigma_\psi^2] \leftarrow [0, I_d]$. Else: $[\mu_\psi(t), \sigma_\psi^2(t)] \leftarrow p_\psi(Z_i(t-1), \{Z_j(t-1)\}_{j \in \mathcal{N}_i(t-1)}, U_i(t))$.
       · Reconstruction: $\log p_\theta(Y_i(t) \mid Z_i(t), U_i(t))$ (computed by decoder).
       · KL divergence: $D_{KL} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=1}^d \left[\log\frac{\sigma_{\psi,j}^2}{\sigma_{\phi,j}^2} + \frac{\sigma_{\phi,j}^2 + (\mu_{\phi,j}-\mu_{\psi,j})^2}{\sigma_{\psi,j}^2} - 1\right]$.
       · $\mathcal{L} \leftarrow \mathcal{L} + \log p_\theta - D_{KL}$.
   · Compute gradient of $-\mathcal{L}/B$ w.r.t. $(\phi,\theta,\psi)$; update with Adam.
3. Posterior extraction: For all $i,t$: $\hat{Z}_i(t) = \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}[Z_i(t)] = \mu_\phi(t)$.

Algorithm C.3: Hazard-Based Forecasting with Model Selection

Input: Latent state estimates $\hat{Z}_i(t)$, raw signals, graph features, threshold $Q^*$, horizon $H$, $\alpha=0.05$, $B_s$ bootstrap samples.

Output: Model $\mathcal{M}^*$, predicted probabilities $\hat{P}(T_i \le H)$ with credible intervals.

1. Feature construction: For each $i,t$:
   · Compute growth rate $g_i(t) = \frac{d}{dt} \log Q_i(t)$ and acceleration $a_i(t) = \frac{d^2}{dt^2} \log Q_i(t)$.
   · Compute graph centrality (e.g., degree, PageRank), bottleneck centrality.
   · Assemble $X_i(t) = [\hat{Z}_i(t), g_i(t), a_i(t), \text{centrality}, \text{bottleneck}, \text{attention features}]$.
   · Compute event time $T_i = \inf\{t: Q_i(t) \ge Q^*\}$ and censoring $\delta_i$.
2. Cox PH fit: Fit $\mathcal{M}_{\text{Cox}}$ on $\{(X_i, T_i, \delta_i)\}$.
3. PH diagnostics: Compute Schoenfeld residuals; perform Grambsch-Therneau test; get $p_{\min}$.
4. Model selection:
   · If $p_{\min} \ge \alpha$: $\mathcal{M}^* \leftarrow \mathcal{M}_{\text{Cox}}$.
   · Else: Train DeepSurv and RSF; evaluate Brier score on validation set; choose best.
5. Prediction: For each test technology $i$ at time $t$:
   · Estimate survival curve $\hat{S}(s \mid X_i(t))$ for $s \in [t, t+H]$.
   · $\hat{P}(T_i \le H) = 1 - \hat{S}(t+H)$.
6. Bootstrap CI: For $b=1,\ldots,B_s$:
   · Resample training data with replacement.
   · Refit selected model; compute $\hat{P}^{(b)}$.
   · Report 5th and 95th percentiles.

