Appendix D: Extension to Discrete Emission Models

The identifiability result of Theorem 4.1 assumes continuous observations with additive noise. In practice, many technology signals are counts (patents, publications), which are non-negative integers. We outline a potential extension to discrete exponential family emissions, adapting the proof of Khemakhem et al. (2020) to discrete likelihoods.

Assumption D.1 (Discrete Exponential Family Emission). The observation $Y \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^{m_c}$ follows a distribution with density (mass function) belonging to an exponential family with natural parameter $\eta(z,u) = h(z,u)$:

p(y \mid z,u) = \exp\left(\langle \eta(z,u), T(y) \rangle - A(\eta(z,u)) + B(y)\right),

where $T(y)$ are sufficient statistics and $A(\eta)$ is the log-partition function.

The key challenge is that the characteristic function approach used for additive noise is not applicable. Instead, identifiability can be established by considering ratios of conditional densities at different auxiliary points, leading to a system of equations involving the sufficient statistics of the latent prior and the emission sufficient statistics. Under conditions similar to Assumptions 4.3–4.4, the same component-wise identifiability can be recovered. The proof involves taking derivatives of the log-density ratio with respect to $u$ and exploiting the linear independence of the functions $T(y)$.

We leave the complete formal proof for future work, noting that the main theoretical contribution remains the continuous case.

